{"authored" [{"question" "q-70917" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine if $v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 7\\end{aligned}].$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "36f25e63bb407b942985c456c9c1193c2c2c26b7d0bcd0c4304ba2d579864652" "basis" 1831 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-70917" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $v$ is an eigenvector, then there must exist a real number $\\lambda $ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ Hence, we have\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=\\lambda [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis leads to the following system of equations for $\\lambda :$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2=2\\lambda  \\\\ 3=-\\lambda \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis system has no solution. Therefore, $v$ is not an eigenvector of $A.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "36f25e63bb407b942985c456c9c1193c2c2c26b7d0bcd0c4304ba2d579864652" "basis" 1831 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-70917" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake