ng"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "36f25e63bb407b942985c456c9c1193c2c2c26b7d0bcd0c4304ba2d579864652" "basis" 1831 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-75735" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/f3030aa3eb132ccd74991bff510393917e83a6683cce37d2c194c1526bef6e90.png"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "36f25e63bb407b942985c456c9c1193c2c2c26b7d0bcd0c4304ba2d579864652" "basis" 1831 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-75735" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/c272ff1454ac4ab9825e312f563c9bca08ac2800faa0e95ab45e8a5e827369da.png"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "36f25e63bb407b942985c456c9c1193c2c2c26b7d0bcd0c4304ba2d579864652" "basis" 1831 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-107837" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "An eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}11 & -6 \\\\ -12 & 25\\end{aligned}]$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =7$ is  $v=[\\begin{aligned}c+1 \\\\ c\\end{aligned}].$ Determine the value of $c.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "36f25e63bb407b942985c456c9c1193c2c2c26b7d0bcd0c4304ba2d579864652" "basis" 1831 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-107837" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\lambda v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}11 & -6 \\\\ -12 & 25\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}c+1 \\\\ c\\end{aligned}] & =7[\\begin{aligned}c+1 \\\\ c\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}5c+11 \\\\ 13c-12\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}7c+7 \\\\ 7c\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}5c+11=7c+7 \\\\ 13c-12=7c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Solving for $c$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n5c+11=7c+7\\,⟹\\,c=2.\n$$\n\n- Solving for $c$ in the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n13c-12=7c\\,⟹\\,c=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution of the system is $c=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "36f25e63bb407b942985c456c9c1193c2c2c26b7d0bcd0c4304ba2d579864652" "basis" 1831 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-107837" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b7ecfca31c0a02c61ffa14aeee33bc80637047bb29b2e10ed4ba4c0d4a20429c" "basis" 1831 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-107837" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "36f25e63bb407b942985c456c9c1193c2c2c26b7d0bcd0c4304ba2d579864652" "basis" 1831 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-107837" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "36f25e63bb407b942985c456c9c1193c2c2c26b7d0bcd0c4304ba2d579864652" "basis" 1831 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-107837" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "36f25e63bb407b942985c456c9c1193c2c2c26b7d0bcd0c4304ba2d579864652" "basis" 1831 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-107837" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "36f25e63bb407b942985c456c9c1193c2c2c26b7d0bcd0c4304ba2d579864652" "basis" 1831 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-107837" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "36f25e63bb407b942985c456c9c1193c2c2c26b7d0bcd0c4304ba2d579864652" "basis" 1831 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-107837" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "36f25e63bb407b942985c456c9c1193c2c2c26b7d0bcd0c4304ba2d579864652" "basis" 1831 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-36678" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $2.$\n- If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2,$ then $-v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-2.$\n- $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "36f25e63bb407b942985c456c9c1193c2c2c26b7d0bcd0c4304ba2d579864652" "basis" 1831 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-36678" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=2v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =2.$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(-v)=-Av=-(2v)=2(-v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(-v)=2(-v),$ and $-v\\ne 0,$ this implies that $-v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =2$ (but not $-2$).\n\n**Note:** In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same.\n\n- Statement III is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =0[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =0v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=0v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "36f25e63bb407b942985c456c9c1193c2c2c26b7d0bcd0c4304ba2d579864652" "basis" 1831 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-36678" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b7ecfca31c0a02c61ffa14aeee33bc80637047bb29b2e10ed4ba4c0d4a20429c" "basis" 1831 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-36678" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "36f25e63bb407b942985c456c9c1193c2c2c26b7d0bcd0c4304ba2d579864652" "basis" 1831 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-36678" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "36f25e63bb407b942985c456c9c1193c2c2c26b7d0bcd0c4304ba2d579864652" "basis" 1831 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-36678" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "36f25e63bb407b942985c456c9c1193c2c2c26b7d0bcd0c4304ba2d579864652" "basis" 1831 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-36678" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "36f25e63bb407b942985c456c9c1193c2c2c26b7d0bcd0c4304ba2d579864652" "basis" 1831 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-36678" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "36f25e63bb407b942985c456c9c1193c2c2c26b7d0bcd0c4304ba2d579864652" "basis" 1831 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-36678" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "None"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "36f25e63bb407b942985c456c9c1193c2c2c26b7d0bcd0c4304ba2d579864652" "basis" 1831 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-36678" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "36f25e63bb407b942985c456c9c1193c2c2c26b7d0bcd0c4304ba2d579864652" "basis" 1831 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-5476" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine if ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/82b99a6e1a271ac0b205a3199b023a582877692c3a61dd4b7144312342fea761.png) is an eigenvector of the matrix ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/2b9e07d0a0089107dc34bb092167719b6e83a4c41ee9f055949a43549449d65e.png) If so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/example-5476.json" "file_sha256" "37718f8c56f156afc6205eadb8b0216ae57b18e7cf10b62741ec05741a79309e" "basis" 1831 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-5476" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $\\mathbf{v}$ such that $A\\mathbf{v}=\\lambda \\mathbf{v}.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =4\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =4v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A\\mathbf{v}=4 \\mathbf{v},$ we conclude that $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda=4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/example-5476.json" "file_sha256" "37718f8c56f156afc6205eadb8b0216ae57b18e7cf10b62741ec05741a79309e" "basis" 1831 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-5891" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/fdfa0528b5476ed0246679c45fd31824470063a68f33c4c9af0f714ad201538f.png)\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of a $2 \\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $2.$ Find the vector $A\\mathbf{v}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/example-5891.json" "file_sha256" "08cf588916e0abecbc72c5e54322b6e437913814079c9399ea0086d8c4cb9f20" "basis" 1831 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-5891" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/bf65510b8cef0e0162e3054bcf31405ac4e4268a759f6493a06c9f0314c1d482.png) corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=2.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av=2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following picture:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/78e1e3c015adad2c826a64e255bd982941dc9fe50679acc2bae3f00b31bd9f39.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/example-5891.json" "file_sha256" "08cf588916e0abecbc72c5e54322b6e437913814079c9399ea0086d8c4cb9f20" "basis" 1831 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-5478" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "An eigenvector of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/22ef70dcb0f7d3cba1c9d5514b40800144eb97edd004041ebb3516062a9e3e48.png) corresponding to an eigenvalue $\\lambda=6c$ is  ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/d1c2b9c36ec942a1c4113f1ff3a6221028f36c6ac4d109351ebab6bb48f358c4.png) Determine the value of $c.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/example-5478.json" "file_sha256" "2cfb09c16dc98bd056192d5c637cfc48223d6f9b54d53df1512fe9852d3f7752" "basis" 1831 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-5478" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $\\mathbf{v}$ such that $A\\mathbf{v}=\\lambda \\mathbf{v}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\lambda v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2c\\end{aligned}] & =6c[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2c\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2+2c \\\\ 1+4c\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6c \\\\ 12{c}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2+2c=6c \\\\ 1+4c=12{c}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $c$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n2+2c=6c \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad c = \\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $c=\\dfrac{1}{2}$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+4c & =12{c}^{2} \\\\ 1+4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} & =12{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\ 3 & =3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a true statement.\n\nTherefore, the solutions of the system is $c=\\dfrac{1}{2}$." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/example-5478.json" "file_sha256" "2cfb09c16dc98bd056192d5c637cfc48223d6f9b54d53df1512fe9852d3f7752" "basis" 1831 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-5477" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/f35353367a395e7b3560aa6f7ef0da0936cefbced9e76d6d7d8f91426a7a1c9b.png) is an eigenvector of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/0b4f33107845bf52d80802844bc6faaec6ab9f4bd159a87838497d2f6abe72d1.png) that corresponds to the eigenvalue $2.$\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/defb480233b4e48f90cb07fef771f2fdb62b6d7d5cb116393d74518eb3b37db8.png) is an eigenvector of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/bb62b77da84018c07d4f3dfefc297684974db78b326bd0736469dc83572ae5b6.png) that corresponds to the eigenvalue $0.$\n\n- If $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda = 5,$ then $2\\mathbf{v}$ is also an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda = 5.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/example-5477.json" "file_sha256" "e3af67a936680cfb49e6e601fea2f4fb0d5c4c54df27e928c8cc395f4693bc25" "basis" 1831 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-5477" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $\\mathbf{v}$ such that $A\\mathbf{v}=\\lambda \\mathbf{v}.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A\\mathbf{v}=2 \\mathbf{v},$ we conclude that $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda=2.$\n\n- Statement II is false. An eigenvector must be a *non-zero* vector.\n\n- Statement III is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(2v)=2Av=2(5v)=5(2v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(2\\mathbf{v})=5( 2 \\mathbf{v} ),$ and $2 \\mathbf{v} \\neq \\mathbf{0},$ this implies that $2 \\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda = 5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is, \"I and III only.\"\n\n**Note:** Statement III is true in the general case, too. In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/example-5477.json" "file_sha256" "e3af67a936680cfb49e6e601fea2f4fb0d5c4c54df27e928c8cc395f4693bc25" "basis" 1831 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-75586" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine if $v=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-75586" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-3v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=-3v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-75586" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "045aeb554329ce2f80fc0e9db69c96f7b19100cbf305a64643fe34ad1ecfbe4e"} {"question" "q-75586" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-75586" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-3"] ["math" "-5"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "5"] ["text" "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-75586" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-106744" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine if $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-106744" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $v$ is an eigenvector, then there must exist a real number $\\lambda $ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ Hence, we have\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=\\lambda [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis leads to the following system of equations for $\\lambda :$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}1=\\lambda  \\\\ -2=-\\lambda \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis system has no solution. Therefore, $v$ is not an eigenvector of $A.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-106744" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "045aeb554329ce2f80fc0e9db69c96f7b19100cbf305a64643fe34ad1ecfbe4e"} {"question" "q-106744" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-106744" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-2"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "4"] ["text" "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-106744" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-70917" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine if $v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 7\\end{aligned}].$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-70917" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $v$ is an eigenvector, then there must exist a real number $\\lambda $ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ Hence, we have\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=\\lambda [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis leads to the following system of equations for $\\lambda :$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2=2\\lambda  \\\\ 3=-\\lambda \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis system has no solution. Therefore, $v$ is not an eigenvector of $A.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-70917" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "045aeb554329ce2f80fc0e9db69c96f7b19100cbf305a64643fe34ad1ecfbe4e"} {"question" "q-70917" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-70917" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-3"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["text" "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-70917" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-106775" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine if $v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}6 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-106775" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $v$ is an eigenvector, then there must exist a real number $\\lambda $ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ Hence, we have\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]=\\lambda [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis leads to the following system of equations for $\\lambda :$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}16=2\\lambda  \\\\ -8=-4\\lambda \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis system has no solution. Therefore, $v$ is not an eigenvector of $A.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-106775" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "045aeb554329ce2f80fc0e9db69c96f7b19100cbf305a64643fe34ad1ecfbe4e"} {"question" "q-106775" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-106775" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-4"] ["math" "-8"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "8"] ["text" "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-106775" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-37002" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine if $v=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}].$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-37002" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ -15\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(-5)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-5v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=-5v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-5.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-37002" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-captu