ion" "value" "The eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Cv & =-4v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following picture:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/assets/99e3776bfa4048703042970a2479a86c749e3e2a547dce966fceb23b671d66f2.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-106890" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "045aeb554329ce2f80fc0e9db69c96f7b19100cbf305a64643fe34ad1ecfbe4e"} {"question" "q-106890" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-106890" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/assets/441a9f0ee158ae2ebdddb223463887ca9f2d5e84a6fc925c80b0aa9ccf4bc65c.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/assets/99e3776bfa4048703042970a2479a86c749e3e2a547dce966fceb23b671d66f2.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/assets/addd53cc78d53e0bb3ee5f5d51128afef8723186516db3aa092dcb97086d616f.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/assets/b595dff95f29976442a7a8fd0acf3baf2f518d364fa55b8e4ffb06e72745260f.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/assets/eb014dbb24f2f087c558d40d2ddb198f225c1ad50a66e6cf5daaaef7eec33021.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-106890" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/assets/99e3776bfa4048703042970a2479a86c749e3e2a547dce966fceb23b671d66f2.png"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-106902" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}k & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2k\\end{aligned}]$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =k+2$ is  $[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ k-1\\end{aligned}].$ Determine the value of $k.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-106902" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\lambda v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}k & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ k-1\\end{aligned}] & =(k+2)[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ k-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-2k+1 \\\\ -2+2{k}^{2}-2k\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-k-2 \\\\ {k}^{2}+k-2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2k+1=-k-2, \\\\ -2+2{k}^{2}-2k={k}^{2}+k-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $k$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n-k=-3\\,⟹\\,k=3.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $k=3$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2+2{k}^{2}-2k & ={k}^{2}+k-2 \\\\ -2+2\\cdot (3)^{2}-2\\cdot 3 & =(3)^{2}+3-2 \\\\ 10 & =10,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a true statement.\n\nTherefore, the solution of the system is $k=3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-106902" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "045aeb554329ce2f80fc0e9db69c96f7b19100cbf305a64643fe34ad1ecfbe4e"} {"question" "q-106902" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-106902" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "10"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-106902" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-107837" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "An eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}11 & -6 \\\\ -12 & 25\\end{aligned}]$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =7$ is  $v=[\\begin{aligned}c+1 \\\\ c\\end{aligned}].$ Determine the value of $c.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-107837" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\lambda v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}11 & -6 \\\\ -12 & 25\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}c+1 \\\\ c\\end{aligned}] & =7[\\begin{aligned}c+1 \\\\ c\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}5c+11 \\\\ 13c-12\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}7c+7 \\\\ 7c\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}5c+11=7c+7 \\\\ 13c-12=7c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Solving for $c$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n5c+11=7c+7\\,⟹\\,c=2.\n$$\n\n- Solving for $c$ in the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n13c-12=7c\\,⟹\\,c=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution of the system is $c=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-107837" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "045aeb554329ce2f80fc0e9db69c96f7b19100cbf305a64643fe34ad1ecfbe4e"} {"question" "q-107837" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-107837" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-107837" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-107842" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -8+k \\\\ 3 & 5k\\end{aligned}]$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2k$ is  $[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Determine the value of $k.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-107842" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\lambda v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & -8+k \\\\ 3 & 5k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =2k[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}k-10 \\\\ -6+5k\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-4k \\\\ 2k\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}k-10=-4k, \\\\ -6+5k=2k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $k$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nk-10=-4k\\,⟹\\,k=2.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $k=2$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6+5k & =2k \\\\ -6+5(2) & =2(2) \\\\ 4 & =4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a true statement.\n\nTherefore, the solution of the system is $k=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-107842" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "045aeb554329ce2f80fc0e9db69c96f7b19100cbf305a64643fe34ad1ecfbe4e"} {"question" "q-107842" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-107842" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-107842" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-75611" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 20-4k & 3\\end{aligned}]$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2k+1$ is  $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Determine the value of $k.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-75611" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\lambda v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 20-4k & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] & =(2k+1)[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 26-4k\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2k+1 \\\\ 4k+2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}7=2k+1, \\\\ 26-4k=4k+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $k$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n7=2k+1\\,⟹\\,k=3.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $k=3$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}26-4k & =4k+2 \\\\ 26-4\\cdot 3 & =4\\cdot 3+2 \\\\ 14 & =14,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a true statement.\n\nTherefore, the solution of the system is $k=3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-75611" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "045aeb554329ce2f80fc0e9db69c96f7b19100cbf305a64643fe34ad1ecfbe4e"} {"question" "q-75611" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-75611" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "-3"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "7"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-75611" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-106945" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "An eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 9 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]$ corresponding to an eigenvalue $\\lambda =6$ is  $[\\begin{aligned}c+32 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}].$ Determine the value of $c.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-106945" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\lambda v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}4 & 9 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}c+32 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] & =6[\\begin{aligned}c+32 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}4c+236 \\\\ 2c+28\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6c+192 \\\\ 72\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4c+236=6c+192 \\\\ 2c+28=72\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Solving for $c$ in the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n2c+28=72\\,⟹\\,c=22.\n$$\n\n- Solving for $c$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n4c+236=6c+192\\,⟹\\,c=22.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution of the system is $c=22.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-106945" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "045aeb554329ce2f80fc0e9db69c96f7b19100cbf305a64643fe34ad1ecfbe4e"} {"question" "q-106945" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-106945" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "12"] ["math" "20"] ["math" "22"] ["math" "26"] ["math" "6"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-106945" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "22"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-107314" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $v$ is an eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{7}$ then $v$ is also an eigenvector of the matrix ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =7.$\n- An eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ is also an eigenvector of the inverse ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =1.$\n- If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2},$ then $-v$ is also an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-107314" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. By the definition of an eigenvector, we have \n\n$$\nAv=\\frac{1}{7}v.\n$$\n\n \nMultiplying it by ${A}^{-1}$ from the left, we obtain \n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{7}{A}^{-1}v.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $7$, we get \n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}v=7v.\n$$\n\n \nSo, $v$ is an eigenvector  of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $7.$\n\n**Note:** In general, an eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to an eigenvalue $\\lambda $ is an eigenvector of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\frac{1}{\\lambda }.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the statement above, an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ is an eigenvector of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{-1}=-1.$\n\n- Statement III is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(-v)=-Av=-(\\frac{1}{2}v)=\\frac{1}{2}(-v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(-v)=\\frac{1}{2}(-v),$ and $-v\\ne 0,$ this implies that $-v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n**Note:** In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-107314" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "045aeb554329ce2f80fc0e9db69c96f7b19100cbf305a64643fe34ad1ecfbe4e"} {"question" "q-107314" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-107314" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-107314" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-75730" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $v$ is an eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{4}$ then $v$ is also an eigenvector of the matrix ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =4.$\n- An eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ is also an eigenvector of the inverse ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the same eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1.$\n- If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5,$ then $-v$ is also an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda =5.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-75730" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. By the definition of an eigenvector, we have \n\n$$\nAv=\\frac{1}{4}v.\n$$\n\n \nMultiplying it by ${A}^{-1}$ from the left, we obtain \n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{4}{A}^{-1}v.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $4$, we get \n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}v=4v.\n$$\n\n \nSo, $v$ is an eigenvector  of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $4.$\n\n**Note:** In general, an eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to an eigenvalue $\\lambda $ is an eigenvector of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\frac{1}{\\lambda }.$\n\n- Statement II is true. According to the statement above, an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ is an eigenvector of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{-1}=-1.$\n\n- Statement III is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(-v)=-Av=-(5v)=5(-v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(-v)=5(-v),$ and $-v\\ne 0,$ this implies that $-v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =5.$\n\n**Note:** In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-75730" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "045aeb554329ce2f80fc0e9db69c96f7b19100cbf305a64643fe34ad1ecfbe4e"} {"question" "q-75730" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-75730" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-75730" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II, and III"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-36678" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $2.$\n- If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2,$ then $-v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-2.$\n- $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-36678" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=2v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =2.$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(-v)=-Av=-(2v)=2(-v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(-v)=2(-v),$ and $-v\\ne 0,$ this implies that $-v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =2$ (but not $-2$).\n\n**Note:** In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same.\n\n- Statement III is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =0[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =0v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=0v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-36678" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "045aeb554329ce2f80fc0e9db69c96f7b19100cbf305a64643fe34ad1ecfbe4e"} {"question" "q-36678" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-36678" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-36678" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-75729" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $v$ is an eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ then $v$ is also an eigenvector of the matrix ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =-2.$\n- An eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ is also an eigenvector of the inverse ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the same eigenvalue $\\lambda =1.$\n- If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2,$ then $7v$ is also an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-75729" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement is false. By the definition of an eigenvector, we have \n\n$$\nAv=2v.\n$$\n\n \nMultiplying it by ${A}^{-1}$ from the left, we obtain \n\n$$\nv=2{A}^{-1}v.\n$$\n\nDividing both sides by $2$, we get \n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}v=\\frac{1}{2}v.\n$$\n\n \nSo, $v$ is an eigenvector  of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n**Note:** In general, an eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to an eigenvalue $\\lambda $ is an eigenvector of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\frac{1}{\\lambda }.$\n\n- Statement II is true. According to the statement above, an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ is an eigenvector of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{1}=1.$\n\n- Statement III is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(7v)=7Av=7(2v)=2(7v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(7v)=2(7v),$ and $7v\\ne 0,$ this implies that $7v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =2.$\n\n**Note:** In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-75729" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "045aeb554329ce2f80fc0e9db69c96f7b19100cbf305a64643fe34ad1ecfbe4e"} {"question" "q-75729" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-75729" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-75729" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-108044" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1.$\n- $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3.$\n- If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2,$ then $3v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-6.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-108044" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $Av=v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =1$ (but not $-1$).\n\n- Statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-3v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $Av=-3v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-3.$\n\n- Statement III is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(3v)=3Av=3(-2v)=-2(3v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(3v)=-2(3v),$ and $3v\\ne 0,$ this implies that $3v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-2$ (but not $-6$).\n\n**Note:** In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-108044" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "045aeb554329ce2f80fc0e9db69c96f7b19100cbf305a64643fe34ad1ecfbe4e"} {"question" "q-108044" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-108044" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "q-108044" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef5a679ffcdac75ed878dede9a7d4cdee6535048833bb5e776280767145c0463"} {"question" "e-5476" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine if ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/assets/82b99a6e1a271ac0b205a3199b023a582877692c3a61dd4b7144312342fea761.png) is an eigenvector of the matrix ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/assets/2b9e07d0a0089107dc34bb092167719b6e83a4c41ee9f055949a43549449d65e.png) If so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/example-5476.json" "file_sha256" "a8c0fd32b613cd9c9ae5e66069dfdfaede5122a2d75632aa90d19c5d7ba8ed92"} {"question" "e-5476" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $\\mathbf{v}$ such that $A\\mathbf{v}=\\lambda \\mathbf{v}.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =4\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =4v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A\\mathbf{v}=4 \\mathbf{v},$ we conclude that $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda=4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/example-5476.json" "file_sha256" "a8c0fd32b613cd9c9ae5e66069dfdfaede5122a2d75632aa90d19c5d7ba8ed92"} {"question" "e-5891" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/assets/fdfa0528b5476ed0246679c45fd31824470063a68f33c4c9af0f714ad201538f.png)\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of a $2 \\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $2.$ Find the vector $A\\mathbf{v}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/example-5891.json" "file_sha256" "d3a104f92201988380f39188353100921fe93910905e22f228d99bcd2d5ed9b9"} {"question" "e-5891" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/assets/9f9c7bc79c4e294e45f34d9e10c0e429c33ce95730d13aaaf337f7a9cb22f1a3.png) corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=2.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av=2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following picture:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/assets/78e1e3c015adad2c826a64e255bd982941dc9fe50679acc2bae3f00b31bd9f39.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/example-5891.json" "file_sha256" "d3a104f92201988380f39188353100921fe93910905e22f228d99bcd2d5ed9b9"} {"question" "e-5478" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "An eigenvector of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/assets/f5ac973d5d7dd390f8cf9231515815f334d0c01dafeab14ba58fef25db4a8e8d.png) corresponding to an eigenvalue $\\lambda=6c$ is  ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/assets/a7e303f19be04db829faaa41133c70d7b6e5647ed75063674ca564a2952c09eb.png) Determine the value of $c.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/example-5478.json" "file_sha256" "37904cb4fa030af77b6c6711b8649195ab4a26af582f2e19df74ccdfa07b0112"} {"question" "e-5478" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $\\mathbf{v}$ such that $A\\mathbf{v}=\\lambda \\mathbf{v}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\lambda v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2c\\end{aligned}] & =6c[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2c\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2+2c \\\\ 1+4c\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6c \\\\ 12{c}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2+2c=6c \\\\ 1+4c=12{c}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $c$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n2+2c=6c \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad c = \\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $c=\\dfrac{1}{2}$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+4c & =12{c}^{2} \\\\ 1+4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} & =12{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\ 3 & =3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a true statement.\n\nTherefore, the solutions of the system is $c=\\dfrac{1}{2}$." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/example-5478.json" "file_sha256" "37904cb4fa030af77b6c6711b8649195ab4a26af582f2e19df74ccdfa07b0112"} {"question" "e-5477" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/assets/f35353367a395e7b3560aa6f7ef0da0936cefbced9e76d6d7d8f91426a7a1c9b.png) is an eigenvector of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/assets/0b4f33107845bf52d80802844bc6faaec6ab9f4bd159a87838497d2f6abe72d1.png) that corresponds to the eigenvalue $2.$\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/assets/ea0bc4d487e57434694dac1835e082f63c9467b496933c8e36770334e77713f5.png) is an eigenvector of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/assets/c87d5c13bbd73d56f44af3ec1b4a867c86e8bf4f709fa869ab8d142424cbe8c4.png) that corresponds to the eigenvalue $0.$\n\n- If $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda = 5,$ then $2\\mathbf{v}$ is also an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda = 5.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/example-5477.json" "file_sha256" "f172633055e79669c177645e927b1e9aa0ee9bb5b1af58d1a657c9b7d511655f"} {"question" "e-5477" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $\\mathbf{v}$ such that $A\\mathbf{v}=\\lambda \\mathbf{v}.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A\\mathbf{v}=2 \\mathbf{v},$ we conclude that $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda=2.$\n\n- Statement II is false. An eigenvector must be a *non-zero* vector.\n\n- Statement III is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(2v)=2Av=2(5v)=5(2v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(2\\mathbf{v})=5( 2 \\mathbf{v} ),$ and $2 \\mathbf{v} \\neq \\mathbf{0},$ this implies that $2 \\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda = 5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is, \"I and III only.\"\n\n**Note:** Statement III is true in the general case, too. In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/example-5477.json" "file_sha256" "f172633055e79669c177645e927b1e9aa0ee9bb5b1af58d1a657c9b7d511655f"}] "usage" {"e-5478" [] "q-36678" [] "q-70917" [] "q-75735" [] "e-5477" [] "q-37091" [] "e-5891" [] "q-107837" [] "q-106744" [] "e-5476" []} "basis" 2553 "retractions" [[17592186333663 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/61ed37aa6a5e67cdc60cca2c93969a47d12e1e36f18edf96f646922877641e79.png)\n\nThe vector $u$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\frac{1}{2}.$ Find the vector $Au.$"] [17592186333663 :question/worked-solution "The eigenvector $u=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au & =\\frac{1}{2}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have the following picture:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/c272ff1454ac4ab9825e312f563c9bca08ac2800faa0e95ab45e8a5e827369da.png)"] [17592186106228 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1375/Source/Images/q-37091.png)\n\nThe vector $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2 \\cdot 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $-1$. Find the vector $Av$."] [17592186105543 :question/problem "Determine if ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/82b99a6e1a271ac0b205a3199b023a582877692c3a61dd4b7144312342fea761.png) is an eigenvector of the matrix ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/2b9e07d0a0089107dc34bb092167719b6e83a4c41ee9f055949a43549449d65e.png) If so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?"] [17592186105551 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/fdfa0528b5476ed0246679c45fd31824470063a68f33c4c9af0f714ad201538f.png)\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of a $2 \\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $2.$ Find the vector $A\\mathbf{v}.$"] [17592186105551 :question/worked-solution "The eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/bf65510b8cef0e0162e3054bcf31405ac4e4268a759f6493a06c9f0314c1d482.png) corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=2.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av=2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following picture:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/78e1e3c015adad2c826a64e255bd982941dc9fe50679acc2bae3f00b31bd9f39.png)"] [17592186105545 :question/problem "An eigenvector of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/22ef70dcb0f7d3cba1c9d5514b40800144eb97edd004041ebb3516062a9e3e48.png) corresponding to an eigenvalue $\\lambda=6c$ is  ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/d1c2b9c36ec942a1c4113f1ff3a6221028f36c6ac4d109351ebab6bb48f358c4.png) Determine the value of $c.$"] [17592186105544 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/f35353367a395e7b3560aa6f7ef0da0936cefbced9e76d6d7d8f91426a7a1c9b.png) is an eigenvector of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/0b4f33107845bf52d80802844bc6faaec6ab9f4bd159a87838497d2f6abe72d1.png) that corresponds to the eigenvalue $2.$\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/defb480233b4e48f90cb07fef771f2fdb62b6d7d5cb116393d74518eb3b37db8.png) is an eigenvector of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/bb62b77da84018c07d4f3dfefc297684974db78b326bd0736469dc83572ae5b6.png) that corresponds to the eigenvalue $0.$\n\n- If $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda = 5,$ then $2\\mathbf{v}$ is also an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda = 5.$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186103878 17592186103879 17592186103880 17592186103881 17592186103882 17592186333546 17592186333547 17592186333548 17592186333549 17592186333550 17592186333588 17592186333589 17592186333590 17592186333591 17592186333592 17592186333602 17592186333603 17592186333604 17592186333605 17592186333606 17592186333630 17592186333631 17592186333632 17592186333633 17592186333634 17592186333665 17592186333666 17592186333667 17592186333668 17592186333669] "immutable_fields" [[175